-"""
-Design analysis for the D3 variant
-==================================
-
-a) Concrete behavior and trace
-YAML-style summary:
-  palette:
-    keep: still a 2xN or Nx2 legend touching one grid edge
-    change:
-      - one extra marker slot is inserted at one end of the palette
-      - that slot contains exactly one bg cell and one unique marker color
-      - the row/column containing the bg marker is the row/column that must be
-        read first; those values are the border-key colors
-      - the opposite row/column gives the fill colors
-    example_horizontal:
-      row0: [0, 4, 7, 2]
-      row1: [9, 6, 1, 3]
-      meaning:
-        - marker slot is the first column [0;9]
-        - bg marker is in row0, so row0 is the border-key line
-        - mapping is 4->6, 7->1, 2->3
-  sprites:
-    keep: several hollow single-color sprites on bg
-    output: enclosed holes whose border color appears in the palette are filled
-
-  trace:
-    original task:
-      - for a top-edge palette, solver assumes top row = border keys,
-        second row = fill colors
-    this variant:
-      - a top-edge palette may instead place the bg marker in the second row
-      - then the second row is the border-key line, independent of edge side
-    visible difference:
-      - two samples can both place the palette on the top edge, but one sample
-        uses the top row as keys and another uses the second row as keys
-
-b) Complexity calibration
-  - Only one new degree of freedom is introduced: which of the two palette
-    lines is read first.
-  - The marker mechanism is repeated across every palette entry because the full
-    mapping still relies on positional pairing after the marked slot.
-  - The marker slot is always unique and visually clean: one bg cell plus one
-    non-bg marker color, with the marker color unused elsewhere.
-  - The palette keeps 2-5 usable mappings, so the task is not reduced to a
-    trivial one-pair lookup.
-
-c) Hidden assumptions in the original main()
-  - `_best_palette(...)` originally accepted palette candidates with no bg
-    inside the block. That assumption is now false because the marker slot must
-    contain a bg cell.
-  - `_palette_mapping(...)` originally hard-coded which row/column was the
-    border line from the palette side. That is exactly the behavior replaced by
-    the marker rule.
-  - `_fill_enclosed_bg_regions(...)` does not depend on the D3 change; once the
-    mapping is recovered, the filling logic remains valid.
-
-d) Placement legality
-  - The generator still uses output-first construction and re-validates with
-    `main(input)`.
-  - The palette occupies a 2-cell-thick edge block with one-cell padding in the
-    occupancy mask, so sprites cannot touch it.
-  - Sprites are placed with the same non-overlap and non-boundary-hole checks as
-    the source task, so the solver sees the same type of enclosed bg regions.
-
-e) Visual observability
-  - The variant is human-visible because the palette now contains an anomalous
-    slot with a bg cell inside an otherwise non-bg legend.
-  - Across samples, the palette can appear on the same edge while the marked
-    row/column switches, forcing the observer to use the marker rather than edge
-    position.
-
-f) Visual cleanliness
-  - Only one marker slot is used.
-  - The marker color is reserved and not reused by sprites or palette values.
-  - The marker slot is placed at one end of the legend to avoid a cluttered
-    middle-of-palette appearance.
-
-g) Color-change inferability
-  - Border keys remain distinct.
-  - Fill values are a derangement of the border keys, so fills are visible and
-    still strongly constrained by the legend.
-  - The marker color never participates in any sprite fill, which keeps the
-    palette semantics readable from 2-4 demonstrations.
-
-Post-change main() assumptions that the generator must guarantee
-  - There is exactly one marker slot in the palette candidate.
-  - All usable palette pairs are fully non-bg.
-  - Border keys are unique and fill colors are unique.
-  - The marker color appears exactly once inside the palette and nowhere else in
-    the grid elements that could masquerade as palette structure.
-"""
